{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794529128912,"sessionID":"ses_e2dbc3ae5ffes1Ef3Xp19Y6IEG","part":{"id":"prt_1d24571d0001B0eCGudaGBGQr4","sessionID":"ses_e2dbc3ae5ffes1Ef3Xp19Y6IEG","messageID":"msg_1d2456a7b001TY7fQNUz3kdJDl","type":"step-start","snapshot":"9c0a0c2c23413c013983c088601ce9f6b3f3c0fd"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794529135060,"sessionID":"ses_e2dbc3ae5ffes1Ef3Xp19Y6IEG","part":{"partID":"prt_1d245746c0015JVL3GUcABkVjA","sessionID":"ses_e2dbc3ae5ffes1Ef3Xp19Y6IEG","messageID":"msg_1d2456a7b001TY7fQNUz3kdJDl","type":"tool","id":"call_52b1f4cfc60343f1b01fcca5","tool":"edit","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md","oldString":"The injected brief begins with `Advisor:` and names the recommended skill(s) with their confidence. A doctor command appears as its runnable `/doctor:` string on the top line or under a skill as an `Also:` second line. The handler returns `{}` when the hook is disabled, input cannot be parsed or an unhandled exception occurs. A shim also returns `{}` when it cannot resolve or relay valid child output, including on timeout. When the handler has no brief, it emits the directives fallback.\n\n---","newString":"The injected brief begins with `Advisor:` and names the routed skill or, when the recommendation resolves to a command, the skill that owns it and the runnable command. A doctor command appears as its runnable `/doctor:` string on the top line or under a skill as an `Also:` second line. The handler returns `{}` when the hook is disabled, input cannot be parsed or an unhandled exception occurs. A shim also returns `{}` when it cannot resolve or relay valid child output, including on timeout. When the handler has no brief, it emits the directives fallback.\n\n### Brief head form\n\nA recommendation that resolves to a command renders one label, shared by the `use` and `ambiguous` heads:\n\n```text\n<ownerSkillId or skill> → <command> (<policy>)\n```\n\nThe recommendation's own `ownerSkillId`, `command` and `policy` are authoritative, and the generated command row fills only what they omit. The policy parenthesis is dropped when no policy is known. A skill with no command row keeps its plain skill label.\n\nThe `use` head reads `Advisor: <freshness>; use <label> <confidence>/<uncertainty> pass.` For example:\n\n```text\nAdvisor: live; use system-spec-kit → /speckit:save (start) 0.89/0.12 pass.\n```\n\nThe ambiguous head applies the same label to both sides: `Advisor: <freshness>; ambiguous: <label> <confidence>/<uncertainty> vs <label> <confidence>/<uncertainty> pass.` Each label part passes `sanitizeSkillLabel`, so a control character, a line break or an instruction-shaped label renders nothing rather than reaching the model. The `Also:` line still names a doctor command by its runnable string.\n\n### Degraded contract\n\nWhen the daemon is unreachable, the CLI runs the local Python scorer and returns `status: ok` with `degraded: true` and `source: local-scorer`. The local scorer returns command row ids, which a reader cannot type, so the CLI rewrites each row before the brief renders:\n\n- A row whose bare slash command appears in the prompt (the row's `command` text before the first space) keeps its command fields: `skillId` is the row id, `kind` is `command`, `command` is the runnable string, `policy` is present when the row has one and `ownerSkillId` names the owning skill. A typed `/speckit:save this session` renders `system-spec-kit → /speckit:save (start)`.\n- Every other row collapses to its owner skill: `skillId` is `ownerSkillId` and `kind` is `skill`. It is dropped when that skill is already offered, so the degraded brief never repeats a skill.\n\nRows below the threshold are discarded before either rule applies. The degraded brief therefore keeps the same head form and names a skill that exists rather than a raw row id.\n\n---"},"output":"Edited .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md (1 replacement)","title":"edit","metadata":{"metadata":{"files":[{"file":".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md","patch":"Index: .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md\n===================================================================\n--- .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md\n+++ .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md\n@@ -39,10 +39,37 @@\n 5. **Render.** `renderAdvisorBrief` produces the model-visible `Advisor:` text. When there is no brief, the handler emits `renderAdvisorFallbackDirective`: one status line, then the directives block. The line reads `Advisor: outage (<status>)` with the command to route by hand when the advisor failed or timed out, `Advisor: no skill matched.` when it answered with nothing above threshold, and `Advisor: prompt skipped.` when the prompt gate declined. The `/doctor:setup` hint appears on no-match and outage lines for prompts that use set up, set-up or setup wording, install or reinstall wording including installation and installer, configure or configuration wording or `check` followed within three words by environment, env, config, hooks, tools, tooling, toolchain or skilled. The no-match line ends with ` For setup or health checks, run /doctor:setup.` The outage line places `for setup or health checks, run /doctor:setup;` before the hand-route command so it stays last. Raw prompt text is never persisted in diagnostics, cache metadata, status, or attribution.\n 6. **Directive-lifecycle dedup.** `decideDirectiveLifecycleDelivery` suppresses a re-delivery of the same directive to the same session within its cadence, so a repeated prompt does not re-inject an identical brief. A repeat in a known session keeps the first line, a route or a fallback status, and drops the directives block.\n 7. **Emit.** Native adapters emit a `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext` JSON envelope. OpenCode appends to `output.system` via `experimental.chat.system.transform`. Pi sends a model-visible message.\n \n-The injected brief begins with `Advisor:` and names the recommended skill(s) with their confidence. A doctor command appears as its runnable `/doctor:` string on the top line or under a skill as an `Also:` second line. The handler returns `{}` when the hook is disabled, input cannot be parsed or an unhandled exception occurs. A shim also returns `{}` when it cannot resolve or relay valid child output, including on timeout. When the handler has no brief, it emits the directives fallback.\n+The injected brief begins with `Advisor:` and names the routed skill or, when the recommendation resolves to a command, the skill that owns it and the runnable command. A doctor command appears as its runnable `/doctor:` string on the top line or under a skill as an `Also:` second line. The handler returns `{}` when the hook is disabled, input cannot be parsed or an unhandled exception occurs. A shim also returns `{}` when it cannot resolve or relay valid child output, including on timeout. When the handler has no brief, it emits the directives fallback.\n \n+### Brief head form\n+\n+A recommendation that resolves to a command renders one label, shared by the `use` and `ambiguous` heads:\n+\n+```text\n+<ownerSkillId or skill> → <command> (<policy>)\n+```\n+\n+The recommendation's own `ownerSkillId`, `command` and `policy` are authoritative, and the generated command row fills only what they omit. The policy parenthesis is dropped when no policy is known. A skill with no command row keeps its plain skill label.\n+\n+The `use` head reads `Advisor: <freshness>; use <label> <confidence>/<uncertainty> pass.` For example:\n+\n+```text\n+Advisor: live; use system-spec-kit → /speckit:save (start) 0.89/0.12 pass.\n+```\n+\n+The ambiguous head applies the same label to both sides: `Advisor: <freshness>; ambiguous: <label> <confidence>/<uncertainty> vs <label> <confidence>/<uncertainty> pass.` Each label part passes `sanitizeSkillLabel`, so a control character, a line break or an instruction-shaped label renders nothing rather than reaching the model. The `Also:` line still names a doctor command by its runnable string.\n+\n+### Degraded contract\n+\n+When the daemon is unreachable, the CLI runs the local Python scorer and returns `status: ok` with `degraded: true` and `source: local-scorer`. The local scorer returns command row ids, which a reader cannot type, so the CLI rewrites each row before the brief renders:\n+\n+- A row whose bare slash command appears in the prompt (the row's `command` text before the first space) keeps its command fields: `skillId` is the row id, `kind` is `command`, `command` is the runnable string, `policy` is present when the row has one and `ownerSkillId` names the owning skill. A typed `/speckit:save this session` renders `system-spec-kit → /speckit:save (start)`.\n+- Every other row collapses to its owner skill: `skillId` is `ownerSkillId` and `kind` is `skill`. It is dropped when that skill is already offered, so the degraded brief never repeats a skill.\n+\n+Rows below the threshold are discarded before either rule applies. The degraded brief therefore keeps the same head form and names a skill that exists rather than a raw row id.\n+\n ---\n \n ## 3. PER-RUNTIME DELIVERY\n \n","status":"modified","additions":28,"deletions":1}],"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Edited .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md (1 replacement)"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md\",\"oldString\":\"The injected brief begins with `Advisor:` and names the recommended skill(s) with their confidence. A doctor command appears as its runnable `/doctor:` string on the top line or under a skill as an `Also:` second line. The handler returns `{}` when the hook is disabled, input cannot be parsed or an unhandled exception occurs. A shim also returns `{}` when it cannot resolve or relay valid child output, including on timeout. When the handler has no brief, it emits the directives fallback.\\n\\n---\",\"newString\":\"The injected brief begins with `Advisor:` and names the routed skill or, when the recommendation resolves to a command, the skill that owns it and the runnable command. A doctor command appears as its runnable `/doctor:` string on the top line or under a skill as an `Also:` second line. The handler returns `{}` when the hook is disabled, input cannot be parsed or an unhandled exception occurs. A shim also returns `{}` when it cannot resolve or relay valid child output, including on timeout. When the handler has no brief, it emits the directives fallback.\\n\\n### Brief head form\\n\\nA recommendation that resolves to a command renders one label, shared by the `use` and `ambiguous` heads:\\n\\n```text\\n<ownerSkillId or skill> → <command> (<policy>)\\n```\\n\\nThe recommendation's own `ownerSkillId`, `command` and `policy` are authoritative, and the generated command row fills only what they omit. The policy parenthesis is dropped when no policy is known. A skill with no command row keeps its plain skill label.\\n\\nThe `use` head reads `Advisor: <freshness>; use <label> <confidence>/<uncertainty> pass.` For example:\\n\\n```text\\nAdvisor: live; use system-spec-kit → /speckit:save (start) 0.89/0.12 pass.\\n```\\n\\nThe ambiguous head applies the same label to both sides: `Advisor: <freshness>; ambiguous: <label> <confidence>/<uncertainty> vs <label> <confidence>/<uncertainty> pass.` Each label part passes `sanitizeSkillLabel`, so a control character, a line break or an instruction-shaped label renders nothing rather than reaching the model. The `Also:` line still names a doctor command by its runnable string.\\n\\n### Degraded contract\\n\\nWhen the daemon is unreachable, the CLI runs the local Python scorer and returns `status: ok` with `degraded: true` and `source: local-scorer`. The local scorer returns command row ids, which a reader cannot type, so the CLI rewrites each row before the brief renders:\\n\\n- A row whose bare slash command appears in the prompt (the row's `command` text before the first space) keeps its command fields: `skillId` is the row id, `kind` is `command`, `command` is the runnable string, `policy` is present when the row has one and `ownerSkillId` names the owning skill. A typed `/speckit:save this session` renders `system-spec-kit → /speckit:save (start)`.\\n- Every other row collapses to its owner skill: `skillId` is `ownerSkillId` and `kind` is `skill`. It is dropped when that skill is already offered, so the degraded brief never repeats a skill.\\n\\nRows below the threshold are discarded before either rule applies. The degraded brief therefore keeps the same head form and names a skill that exists rather than a raw row id.\\n\\n---\"}"},"time":{"start":1794529129580,"end":1794529135060}}}}
